From fa3880b1c5047d8c3cbb8f3eaab9a551f7bd0ce7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: nicktoper <ntoper@manycore.io>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 12:38:18 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] Write autoclose retire follow-ups to a durable worklist
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

`_report_retire_followups` sent its report to `blackboard_from_env`, i.e.
whatever `COGA_TASK_BLACKBOARD` names. For the `autoclose-merged` recurring
template that is the *period* task, which `coga recurring` deletes at the
start of the next period — so the sweep's one actionable output was written
to a file scheduled for deletion. `render_retire_report`'s own docstring
assumed the opposite ("a long-lived recurring task's blackboard"), which is
how the bug survived review.

A recurring run now records structured, slug-keyed entries in the template's
durable `retires.md`, a sibling of `ticket.md` that no period boundary
touches. Re-recording a slug refreshes the checkout it names and keeps the
first sighting's date, so a template that strands the same ticket on
consecutive runs accumulates one entry rather than one per run. A malformed
or foreign worklist fails loudly instead of growing a second section no
reader would find.

Non-recurring runs are unchanged: the task blackboard when there is one,
stdout otherwise. The skill, the shipped recurring template, and its
dogfooded copy now all name the durable path instead of "the task
blackboard", which was the ambiguity that pointed the write at the period
task in the first place.

